<DocsPage page="docs/history/" title="How Agent Codeyard began" description="How Agent Codeyard began, told by its maker, and what comes next; then period by period with the pull requests that built it, and the frameworks and talks it learned from." toc={toc} class="history">
  <span id="phases"></span><span id="principles"></span><span id="decisions"></span>
  <p>I started Agent Codeyard with a question about which AI model to use for which part of building software. I wanted to use a model like Fable for the hardest planning decisions, and a model like Opus to implement individual features once the plan was clear. That raised a second question. Once an agent had built a feature, how would I know it was ready?</p>

  <h2 id="giving-each-agent-a-job">Giving each agent a job</h2>
  <p>I began with a simple routine. I put the feature in a GitHub issue, had one agent implement it, and asked a separate agent to review the changes. A review could produce several findings, and each needed a decision. Some pointed to bugs worth fixing immediately. Others needed more evidence or could wait. I added a step to make those decisions explicit, then sent the agreed fixes back to the implementer. That gave me a repeatable cycle for building, reviewing, and revising a feature.</p>
  <p>The routine worked when I already knew what I wanted to build. For a new project, I often needed to work that out first. I added Project Grill, a conversation in which an agent asks questions about the idea, challenges assumptions, and helps me turn the answers into requirements. A planning agent could then use those requirements to propose an architecture and a roadmap. Later, I gave the plan its own independent review and revision. That let me catch problems in the plan before they became problems in the code.</p>

  <h2 id="making-the-handoffs-reliable">Making the handoffs reliable</h2>
  <p>As I used the workflow more, I spent more time passing information from one step to the next. The reviewer needed to know what the implementer had changed and checked. The agent deciding what to do with the review findings needed to know exactly what the reviewer had found. The implementer then needed to know which fixes to make.</p>
  <p>I had been using Markdown for review findings and decisions. It was easy to read, but scripts had to interpret the text to figure out what should happen next. I moved those records to structured JSON so the scripts could validate them and match each decision to the right finding. I also tied reviews and verification results to the version of the code they covered. If the code changed, the workflow could tell which results were still valid.</p>
  <p>GitHub became the place where I could follow the work. I could turn a roadmap item into a feature issue, and the workflow could post readable review findings and decisions back to that issue. If I wanted to know why a fix had been made or why a finding was left open, I could follow the discussion in one place.</p>

  <h2 id="from-an-issue-to-a-pull-request">From an issue to a pull request</h2>
  <p>By then I was running much the same sequence for each feature. I wanted to start it with one command and have the workflow pause whenever it needed a decision from me. Once the scripts could tell whether the checks had passed and whether the review covered the current code, they could also decide when to continue or retry. An interrupted session could pick up from a handoff note without needing the original chat.</p>
  <p>Eventually, one command could take a feature from its GitHub issue through implementation, verification, review, and revision to an open pull request. I used the pull request to inspect the changes, check the verification and review results, and resolve any open decisions before deciding whether to merge.</p>

  <h2 id="learning-from-real-projects">Learning from real projects</h2>
  <p>Using the workflow on real projects showed me where it asked for too much work. A small documentation edit could go through much the same process as a change to the scripts that decide whether a review is valid. I started recording how long runs took, which models they used, and where I had to step in. That gave me something concrete to look at when a change took longer than I expected.</p>
  <p>I added risk levels so the amount of verification and review could follow the consequences of the change. Small edits could take a lighter route, while changes with a wider impact could get more scrutiny. Risk also gave me a better way to make the model choice I had started with. I could decide where a more expensive model was worth using based on the work in front of it. Later, I added a way to bring the workflow into an existing repository, starting with the project's own conventions and checks.</p>
  <p>What began as a choice between models became a way to work with coding agents across the software development lifecycle. I can start with an idea I'm still working through, follow it into a plan and a set of changes, and see the evidence I need to decide whether those changes are ready to merge.</p>

  <h2 id="what-comes-next">What comes next</h2>
  <p>Today I still start each feature run myself, answer the questions it stops for, and decide every merge. Several runs can work side by side, so my work has moved from driving each agent to overseeing the runs. The next step is a supervisor: a layer above the runs that starts and follows the features of an approved plan and brings me only the decisions. I also want Agent Codeyard to work with open-source models that run on your own machine, through opencode and Ollama, beside Claude Code and Codex. What stays with me is what I started with: deciding what gets built and how, steering now and then, and deciding what gets merged.</p>

  <h2 id="history">History</h2>
  <p>Period by period, with the pull requests that built it. Pages are named as they were called then: Examples is now Projects, and Workflow is How it works; Features, called Why Codeyard for a while, has its name again.</p>
